#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Year 3 Creative Arts Written Core batch 02: pitch, dynamics, tempo, listening, role, voice w07-l1 through w12-l2 (12)."""
from arts_support import L, pv, create, MUS, DRA

LESSONS = [
L('s2-arts-y3-w07-l1', MUS, ['pitch', 'high and low', 'a rising melody', 'a falling melody'],
  "Pitch is how high or low a sound is. A small bird chirp is high pitched and a big drum or a tuba is low pitched. A melody is a tune made by changing pitch. When the notes move up, the melody is rising, and when they move down it is falling. If the notes stay at the same pitch, the melody is repeating. You can show pitch with your hand moving up and down or with a line going up and down on paper. Pitch is different from volume, which is how loud or soft a sound is.",
  "Example: In the song 'Hot Cross Buns' the first three notes fall: 'Hot cross buns', so the melody falls and the word 'buns' is the lowest note. In the playground, a swing going up is like a rising melody, and when it comes down it is like a falling one. A tiny whistle has high pitch while a big truck horn has a low pitch, even if the whistle is much quieter.",
  "A mouse squeaks, a cow moos and a bird tweets. Order them from highest to lowest pitch, and say whether a melody that goes from the cow to the bird is rising or falling.",
  "Strong answer: highest is the bird, then the mouse, then the cow is lowest. Going from the cow (low) to the bird (high) is a rising melody. Pitch means high or low, so a loud sound can still be low pitched.",
  "Thinking that high pitch means loud and low pitch means quiet. Pitch and loudness are separate, because a quiet whistle is still high and a loud drum is still low.",
  "Make a sound map: draw six animals or objects and write high, medium or low under each. Then hum a rising melody and a falling melody and draw each as a line going up or down. Teach someone at home the difference between pitch and volume."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w07-l2', MUS, ['a pitch game', 'a siren', 'matching pitch', 'a warm-up'],
  "Singing is easier and safer for your voice when you warm up first with gentle sounds. Try a siren, sliding your voice from low to high and back with an 'oo' sound, and humming. To match pitch, listen first to the note, think of it in your head and then sing it back. Sing at a comfortable volume, stand tall with relaxed shoulders and sip water. Never shout or strain, and stop if your throat feels sore. If you are not comfortable singing, you can hum or tap the pitch pattern.",
  "Example: Ravi plays a pitch echo game with his sister. She hums a low note, and he listens, then hums it back. Next she sings a high note, then a middle note, then a low note, and he copies the pattern. If his note is too low, he listens again and moves his voice up a little until the sounds match.",
  "Your partner sings 'low, high, high' and you need to match it. Write what you will do before singing, and how you will fix it if your note is wrong.",
  "Strong answer: 'I will listen to all three notes first, think them in my head, then sing low, high, high. If my high note is too low, I will listen again and slide my voice up a little.' Listening before singing and adjusting is how pitch matching works.",
  "Thinking that you must sing loudly or high to be good at singing. Good singing is gentle, in tune and in time, and shouting can hurt your voice.",
  "Play a pitch echo game with someone at home with five short patterns using high, middle and low notes. Write each pattern as H, M or L. Then do a siren warm-up and describe how your voice moved. Stop if your voice feels strained."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w08-l1', MUS, ['dynamics', 'piano (soft)', 'forte (loud)', 'crescendo'],
  "Dynamics are how loud or soft music is. The word piano, written p, means soft, and forte, written f, means loud. A crescendo is a gradual getting louder, shown with a widening shape, and a diminuendo is a gradual getting softer. Changing dynamics makes music exciting, like a story where something is far away and then gets closer. Composers write dynamics on the music so players know when to change. Remember that soft does not mean slow and loud does not mean fast.",
  "Example: a piece starts piano with quiet taps like footsteps far away. The taps get louder with a crescendo as someone comes closer, reach forte when the person is at the door and then quickly drop back to piano for a whispered knock. The tempo stays the same all the way through; only the loudness changes.",
  "Plan a short piece about a thunderstorm. Where would you use piano, forte, crescendo and diminuendo? Write the dynamics in order.",
  "Strong answer: 'Piano for the first quiet drips, a crescendo as the storm comes closer, forte for the loud thunder and a diminuendo as it moves away.' Each dynamic matches a part of the story.",
  "Thinking that dynamics mean fast and slow. Dynamics are about loud and soft only, and changing the speed is a different idea called tempo.",
  "Choose a short rhyme or song and write a dynamics plan with p, f, a crescendo and a diminuendo above the words. Perform it using your voice or body percussion at a safe volume, and explain how the dynamic changes make it more interesting."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w08-l2', MUS, ['a shaker', 'a drum', 'a soft touch', 'a strong touch'],
  "On classroom instruments, the way you play changes the dynamics. On a drum, a gentle tap with a soft mallet is piano and a firmer hit is forte. Shaking a shaker slowly makes it quiet and shaking faster makes it louder. Use the right part of the mallet, hold it loosely and strike the middle of the drum. Always play only when the leader says go and stop together on a clear signal. Instruments are not for throwing, and you can use household items such as a plastic tub as a drum.",
  "Example: the leader holds up a flat hand for piano and a raised hand for forte. The class plays a shaker pattern: shake, shake, rest. When the hand is low, shakers are soft. As the hand rises slowly, the shakes get louder. At the top they are forte, and when the hand comes down they get softer again. The beat never changes.",
  "You are the leader of a group of drummers. Make a plan using hand signals to ask for soft, crescendo, loud and a stop. Write the signals and say how the players know when to stop.",
  "Strong answer: 'Flat low hand = soft, hand rising slowly = crescendo, high open hand = loud, closed fist = stop. Players watch the leader and stop together at the fist.' Clear signals keep the group together and safe.",
  "Believing that louder always sounds better. Controlling the dynamics, including playing very quietly, takes more skill than banging, and too much noise hides the other players.",
  "Make a dynamics piece for two instruments (or household items such as a tub and a container of rice). Write a plan using p, f, crescendo and diminuendo, perform it with a family member and write how the dynamics changed the mood."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w09-l1', MUS, ['tempo', 'allegro (fast)', 'adagio (slow)', 'accelerando'],
  "Tempo is the speed of the beat. A fast tempo has many beats a minute and a slow tempo has fewer. Musicians use Italian words: allegro means fast and lively, adagio means slow and calm. An accelerando is a gradual speeding up and a ritardando is a gradual slowing down. Tempo changes the mood: fast music can feel excited or busy, and slow music can feel peaceful or sad. A steady beat at any tempo keeps the music together, and tempo does not change how high or loud the notes are.",
  "Example: a lullaby is played at a slow tempo of around 60 beats a minute to feel sleepy. A galloping horse piece is allegro, about 120 beats a minute, and feels exciting. In a piece about a train, the beats begin slowly with a ritardando at the station and gradually get faster with an accelerando as the train speeds up.",
  "Choose a tempo word (allegro, adagio, accelerando or ritardando) for each: a snail, a cheetah, a bus pulling away from the stop and a bus arriving at the stop.",
  "Strong answer: snail is adagio, cheetah is allegro, a bus pulling away is accelerando because it is getting faster, and a bus arriving is ritardando because it is slowing down. Tempo is about speed, which can change gradually.",
  "Confusing tempo with dynamics. A quiet song can be fast and a loud song can be slow, because tempo is the speed of the beat and dynamics are how loud it is.",
  "Choose four songs or pieces and write the tempo word that fits each, with a reason. Then tap a steady beat at a slow tempo and a fast tempo, and then do an accelerando. Describe how each tempo changes the mood."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w09-l2', MUS, ['a tempo change', 'a cue', 'a conductor', 'staying together'],
  "When a group performs with a tempo change, players need to stay together. The conductor gives a clear cue, such as a hand motion, before a change. All players listen carefully and follow the leader's beat rather than their own speed. A good performance starts with a count-in, such as '1, 2, 3, 4', so everyone starts at the same time. If a mistake happens, keep going with the beat. It is better to stay together than to stop and restart.",
  "Example: a class performs a clapping piece with three sections. Section A is adagio and Section B is allegro. The conductor lifts the baton quickly to show 'faster' before B, and the class follows. They count in '1, 2, 3, 4' before each section. Zoe loses her place but keeps clapping the beat and rejoins at the next section.",
  "A group is playing a piece that speeds up in the middle, but one player keeps playing slowly. Write what the player should do, and what the leader can do to help.",
  "Strong answer: 'The player should watch the conductor and listen to the others to follow the beat, and the leader can use bigger hand motions and count in again.' Following the leader and listening are what keep a group together.",
  "Thinking that you should stop and start again whenever you make a mistake. In a group performance, keep playing the beat and rejoin so the music keeps going.",
  "Write a three-part clapping piece: slow, medium, fast. Add a cue for each change, such as a raised hand. Perform it with a family member as the conductor, then swap roles. Write what made it easy or hard to stay together."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w10-l1', MUS, ['listening', 'an element of music', 'a mood', 'a music response'],
  "Listening to music carefully means noticing the elements, such as beat, rhythm, pitch, dynamics, tempo and instruments, and how they make the piece feel. Listen once for the mood, and again for the elements. Describe what you hear and then say how it makes you feel, giving a reason from the music, such as 'The slow tempo and soft dynamics make it feel calm.' Different people may feel different things. It is respectful to listen to the whole piece quietly before responding.",
  "Example: Ben listens to a short piece about a storm. First time: 'It feels scary.' Second time: 'The tempo is fast and the drums are forte, so it feels exciting and scary. The high pitched flute sounds like lightning.' He names tempo, dynamics, instruments and pitch to explain the mood.",
  "A piece is played slowly on a low cello and gets softer at the end. Describe two elements you hear and say what mood they might create.",
  "Strong answer: 'The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.' Each element is linked to a feeling, which is a stronger response than just 'it is sad'.",
  "Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear.",
  "Listen to three short pieces with an adult, such as a lullaby, a march and a piece from your own choice. For each write the mood, two elements you heard and one reason. Draw a picture of the mood for your favourite."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w10-l2', MUS, ['a showcase', 'a performance plan', 'feedback', 'a reflection'],
  "At the end of a music unit, musicians share what they have learned in a short showcase and then reflect on it. A good plan names the piece, the elements that will be used (beat, rhythm, pitch, dynamics, tempo), the order of parts and a signal to start. After the performance, the audience claps and gives feedback as two stars and a wish. The performer then writes a reflection on what went well and what they will practise next.",
  "Example: Mina's showcase has three parts: a clapped rhythm, a rising and falling hum and a shaker piece with a crescendo. She names each element before she performs. A listener says, 'Star: the crescendo was smooth. Star: your beat was steady. Wish: sing the high note a little louder.' Her reflection says to practise the high note.",
  "Plan a one-minute showcase using two music elements from this unit. Write the order and a start signal.",
  "Strong answer: 'First: clap a four-beat rhythm twice. Second: hum a rising melody with a crescendo. Start signal: nod and count 1, 2, 3, 4.' The plan names two elements, an order and a signal.",
  "Believing that a performance has to be perfect. A showcase shows learning, and a mistake you recover from is part of performing.",
  "Prepare a short showcase with at least three elements from this unit, perform it for family and ask for two stars and a wish. Write a reflection about what went well and one thing to practise next."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w11-l1', DRA, ['a role', 'a character', 'a character trait', 'staying in role'],
  "In drama, a role is a person or creature you pretend to be. A character is built from traits, such as brave, shy or grumpy, and from what the character wants. You show a character through your body (how you stand and move), your face and your voice. Staying in role means you keep acting as the character until the teacher or leader says to stop. Drama is pretend and safe, with no hitting or real touching. You can always step out of a role or describe it if you do not want to act.",
  "Example: Zoe plays an old fisherman. She decides his traits are patient and tired. She walks slowly with a bent back, speaks in a low, slow voice and sighs. Her character wants to catch one last fish. Everyone can tell the character's traits from her body and voice, and she keeps in role until the teacher says 'freeze and thank you'.",
  "Create a character who is a nervous new student. Choose two traits and say how you would show them with your body, face and voice.",
  "Strong answer: 'Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.' It links each trait to a physical choice, which makes a character believable.",
  "Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character's traits so the audience can believe in them.",
  "Make three characters: a king, a robot and a shy mouse. For each write two traits, a way of standing and moving, a face and a voice. Perform one for a family member who guesses the traits. You may describe instead of performing."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w11-l2', DRA, ['a freeze frame', 'a still image', 'a role play', 'a thought bubble'],
  "A freeze frame, also called a still image, is a pose where actors hold still like a photograph to show a moment in a story. Choose a moment, decide who each person is, and show it with body shape, facial expression and the distance between people. Hold still for five seconds and breathe quietly. A thought bubble is when the leader taps a person and they say what their character is thinking. Be careful, and never touch or push others.",
  "Example: a group makes a freeze frame of 'the lost kitten found'. One child kneels with arms out, wide eyes and a smile. Another points. A third stands with hands on cheeks looking relieved. When the teacher taps the first child, she says, 'I thought I'd never see you again!' The pose and the thought show the moment clearly.",
  "Plan a freeze frame of 'the winning goal' for a group of three. Say who each person is and what pose and face each would show.",
  "Strong answer: 'One player in a kicking pose with a proud face, one with both arms up cheering, one goalkeeper diving with a shocked face.' Different roles at different levels make the image clear, and each pose shows an emotion.",
  "Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling. A good still image shows the moment and feelings through body shape and expression, and every person has a role.",
  "Create three freeze frames for a story, such as the beginning, middle and end of a picnic. Draw or photograph each, write a thought bubble for one character in each and perform them for family. Stay safe and do not touch others."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w12-l1', DRA, ['volume', 'pace', 'gesture', 'facial expression'],
  "In drama, voice and body work together to show a character. Voice has volume (loud or soft), pace (fast or slow), pitch (high or low) and tone (the feeling in the voice, such as friendly or angry). Body has posture (how you stand), gesture (hand and arm movements) and facial expression. Change these on purpose. A scared character might speak quickly and softly while a giant might speak slowly and loudly. Use a clear voice so the audience can hear, but never shout or strain your throat.",
  "Example: for a giant, Omar speaks slowly in a deep, loud voice and takes big stomping steps with his arms wide. For a mouse, he speaks quickly in a high, soft voice with tiny steps and his hands held close. The changes in volume, pace, pitch and posture make two clearly different characters.",
  "Say 'Come here, please.' in three ways: as a worried parent, an excited friend and a sleepy grandfather. Describe the pace, volume and body for each.",
  "Strong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and soft with a yawn. The same words change meaning when voice and body change.",
  "Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout.",
  "Choose a sentence of your own and write four ways to say it with different volume, pace, pitch and body choices. Perform two of them for someone and ask them to guess the character or feeling."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w12-l2', DRA, ['a warm-up', 'a mirror game', 'an echo game', 'freeze'],
  "Drama games warm up the voice and body and build trust. In a mirror game, partners face each other and one leads slow movements while the other copies as if in a mirror. In an echo game, a leader says a phrase in a particular voice and the group repeats it exactly. In a freeze game, everyone moves in a clear space until the leader says freeze, then holds still. Move slowly in a safe space and keep your hands to yourself. Anyone can sit out and watch or lead.",
  "Example: In the mirror game, Lee raises a hand slowly, and Jo raises the opposite hand to match. Lee turns slowly and Jo turns with him. They keep eye contact and move at a speed where the movement looks like a reflection. When the teacher says freeze, both stop and hold still. Good games use slow, smooth movements.",
  "In a mirror game, your partner moves very fast and you cannot keep up. Write what you can say or do to fix this, and one safety rule for the game.",
  "Strong answer: 'I will ask my partner to slow down. Slow movements are easier to copy and look better. Rule: keep a safe space between us and do not touch.' Speaking up kindly and slowing down keeps the game fun and safe.",
  "Believing that a drama game is a race. The aim is to work together and make clear, controlled movement, so slower is usually better.",
  "Play three games with a family member: mirror, echo and freeze. Write the rules you used and one safety rule for each. Record how well you stayed together from 1 to 5 and one change that would make the game better."),
]

